title:   Finding a bug the assembler cannot see
level:   after the first program
needs:   assembler, emulator, rom
learn:   reading a loop's real behaviour out of the registers instead of the comments
source:  file fault.s

step  Assemble the suspect
  teach  fault.s is a small program with a comment that lies. It says it adds 10
         to D1. The assembler has no complaint, because nothing here is illegal —
         which is the kind of bug you cannot find by reading.
  do     build
  expect problems == none

step  Run it
  teach  {key:run} stops at the entry point before anything has run, so the
         registers still hold whatever TOS left there.
  do     run
  expect stopped

step  Catch the loop
  teach  Put a breakpoint on the `dbf` line — line 21 — and Continue. It fires
         before the instruction runs, so you are looking at the loop's first pass:
         D0 still holds the count, exactly as the `moveq` set it.
  do     breakpoint 21
  do     continue
  expect stopped
  expect at fault.s:21
  expect reg.d0 == 10

step  Count the passes
  teach  `dbf` decrements first and loops while the counter is not -1, so counting
         10 down to -1 runs the body eleven times. Clear the breakpoint on the loop
         line first — continuing from a breakpoint the loop revisits would stop on
         the same line again — then break on the `clr.w` at line 23 and Continue:
         the loop finishes and stops there, with the total the comment promised to
         make 10 sitting in D1 as 11. That is the bug, and you found it by watching
         the machine rather than trusting the source.
  do     clearbreakpoint 21
  do     breakpoint 23
  do     continue
  expect stopped
  expect at fault.s:23
  expect reg.d1 == 11
